@kalpanatpm The number one reason in my experience for not being able to finish stories in a given sprint is because it was poorly scoped as in poorly understood by engineering and not broken down into several small stories and therefore not pointed appropriately. Pointing at anything over 8 points just exhibits ambiguity about the story. I have gone to the extent of splitting stories to call out backend API being one story as an example, documentation for it being another story, integration of the API with another API as yet another story and eventually customer communication being another story. This kind of splitting has been clear to the team with clear goals. And none has exceeded 5 points. 
Once story definition is clear, team moves forward quickly.  It's imperative to have discussion post sprint planning too if needed. I have encouraged focussed time together with members on specific stories to clear ambiguity and this has resulted in clear goals and reduced scope by moving an ambigious aspect of the story to a future sprint. 
Unclear aspects often come up during standups and it's critical to address these either at standup itself or immediately after with affected folks. Engaging with Product Manager to descope is equally important.
